before:
This complete example updates an existing key and inserts a missing one without removing the unrelated preference.
```sql
CREATE TABLE demo_settings(key TEXT PRIMARY KEY NOT NULL,value TEXT NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO demo_settings VALUES('sound','on'),('font','large');
INSERT INTO demo_settings(key,value) VALUES('sound','off') ON CONFLICT(key) DO UPDATE SET value=excluded.value;
INSERT INTO demo_settings(key,value) VALUES('alerts','on') ON CONFLICT(key) DO UPDATE SET value=excluded.value;
SELECT key,value
FROM demo_settings
ORDER BY key;
```
The rows are alerts/on, font/large and sound/off.

after:
Run this example in a fresh database:
```sql
CREATE TABLE demo_settings (
  key TEXT PRIMARY KEY NOT NULL,
  value TEXT NOT NULL
);
INSERT INTO demo_settings VALUES ('sound', 'on'), ('font', 'large');
INSERT INTO demo_settings(key, value)
VALUES ('sound', 'off'), ('alerts', 'on')
ON CONFLICT(key) DO UPDATE SET value = excluded.value;
SELECT key, value
FROM demo_settings
ORDER BY key;
```
The rows are alerts/on, font/large, and sound/off.
